{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at 58c804aa730baa6025fa3fd88c16b96bd4eba6e0: the sequencer's overload refusal.\n\nOver a configured MaxQueueDepth, Submitter.Submit returns ErrBackPressure after one atomic add taken before the lock. A refused submission parses no intent, never reaches the pre-append hook, writes no payload tree, and never chains — the refusal precedes all of it rather than following the expensive part, which is what makes it a signal a caller can act on. Zero remains unbounded: the embedding opt-out for a kernel used outside this application.\n\nRotation reports its own anonymous exhaustion result, unchanged. An earlier head classified it as capacity and proved that with a negative retry count, which runs the loop zero times and so shows only that an invalid configuration returns the sentinel. The alternative was a production failpoint inside Rotate existing solely so a test could reach the line, and a seam that exists for tests is worse than the classification it would prove.\n\nTests, each checked against the mutation it exists for:\n- Moving the gate after the lock — where it still refuses, but only after queuing — fails the capacity test. That is what separates \"before chaining\" from \"returns an error\".\n- The first arrival is bounded, so a wrongly refused first slot fails with a diagnostic instead of hanging to the test timeout.\n- The unbounded case queues four followers, not one; one follower shows only that nothing was refused, which a limit of two would also satisfy.\n\nQueueDepth is new: it reports the bound this submitter was built with, so a deployment can prove its posture rather than trust that the option was passed. It exists because an earlier head shipped an option nobody set, and the app-side regression test needs to read the constructed object.\n\nThe tree here is byte-identical to the head that already passed the full gates; only the commit and its basis differ.\n\nWHY THIS REPLACES AN ARTIFACT PUBLISHED MINUTES AGO. I retired candidate fda8bc9b, and the authorization chain for that work descended from it, so retirement staled the chain retroactively and the artifacts under it were stale from birth. Nothing here rests on any of that lineage. The history is recorded in prose rather than cited, precisely so the staleness does not travel back in.\n\nThis rests on its authorizing request, my promise, and d97eed89, the live artifact for what main carries at this path. That shipped predecessor stays live until a guarded merge.","body":{"commit":"58c804aa730baa6025fa3fd88c16b96bd4eba6e0","path":"internal/kernel"}}